1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,760
 Large language models are powerful, but they operate within a fundamental constraint.

2
00:00:05,760 --> 00:00:09,920
 They generate responses based on patterns learned during training,

3
00:00:09,920 --> 00:00:13,280
 not by querying live systems or verifying facts.

4
00:00:13,280 --> 00:00:18,560
 This means that while their answers often sound confident and well-structured,

5
00:00:18,560 --> 00:00:22,880
 they may be outdated, incomplete, or entirely fabricated,

6
00:00:22,880 --> 00:00:25,680
 when asked about information beyond their training data.

7
00:00:26,640 --> 00:00:30,240
 In business environments, this limitation becomes dangerous.

8
00:00:30,240 --> 00:00:33,840
 Organizations rely on constantly changing data.

9
00:00:33,840 --> 00:00:39,040
 Policies evolve, products update, customers generate new records,

10
00:00:39,040 --> 00:00:41,120
 and systems log real-time events.

11
00:00:41,120 --> 00:00:45,440
 A model that cannot see this data is forced to guess.

12
00:00:45,440 --> 00:00:50,080
 This is why LLMs alone are not enough for production systems.

13
00:00:50,080 --> 00:00:52,960
 They excel at language, not truth verification.

14
00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:57,920
 Retrieval augmented generation or rag addresses this gap

15
00:00:57,920 --> 00:01:01,600
 by grounding AI responses in actual up-to-date data

16
00:01:01,600 --> 00:01:02,960
 retrieve that query time.

17
00:01:02,960 --> 00:01:08,000
 Instead of guessing, the model reasons over evidence provided by your systems.

18
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:13,200
 This shift, from language generation to evidence-based generation,

19
00:01:13,200 --> 00:01:16,240
 is what makes AI usable in real businesses.

20
00:01:16,240 --> 00:01:20,080
 Every LLM has a fixed knowledge cutoff,

21
00:01:20,080 --> 00:01:23,120
 a point in time after which it stops learning.

22
00:01:23,120 --> 00:01:27,520
 Anything that happens after that date is invisible to the model.

23
00:01:27,520 --> 00:01:30,960
 New policies, updated documentation,

24
00:01:30,960 --> 00:01:36,720
 regulatory changes, or internal decisions simply do not exist from the model's perspective.

25
00:01:36,720 --> 00:01:41,120
 When users ask questions about post-cut-off information,

26
00:01:41,120 --> 00:01:43,200
 the model doesn't say "I don't know."

27
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:47,360
 Instead, it predicts what a plausible answer might look like.

28
00:01:47,920 --> 00:01:51,200
 This behavior creates a dangerous illusion of accuracy.

29
00:01:51,200 --> 00:01:56,960
 Rag directly solves this problem by injecting current information at runtime.

30
00:01:56,960 --> 00:02:00,240
 Instead of relying on frozen training data,

31
00:02:00,240 --> 00:02:04,160
 the system retrieves relevant documents from your live knowledge sources

32
00:02:04,160 --> 00:02:05,840
 and supplies them as context.

33
00:02:05,840 --> 00:02:12,000
 The model then generates answers grounded in what your organization actually knows today,

34
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:14,720
 not what the internet looked like months or years ago.

35
00:02:15,440 --> 00:02:20,160
 This makes Rag essential for any system that must reflect current reality

36
00:02:20,160 --> 00:02:22,080
 rather than historical snapshots.

37
00:02:22,080 --> 00:02:28,000
 A common misconception is that fine-tuning a model will solve the knowledge problem.

38
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:32,080
 In reality, fine-tuning introduces new challenges.

39
00:02:32,080 --> 00:02:36,480
 It is expensive, slow, and operationally complex.

40
00:02:36,480 --> 00:02:40,640
 Updating a fine-tuned model can take weeks or months,

41
00:02:40,640 --> 00:02:44,480
 making it unsuitable for fast-changing business environments.

42
00:02:45,440 --> 00:02:49,200
 More importantly, fine-tuning does not eliminate hallucinations.

43
00:02:49,200 --> 00:02:54,640
 Even a fine-tuned model will fabricate answers when information is missing.

44
00:02:54,640 --> 00:02:59,520
 You're still relying on internalized patterns rather than verifying sources.

45
00:02:59,520 --> 00:03:02,800
 Rag offers a better architectural approach.

46
00:03:02,800 --> 00:03:08,640
 Knowledge stays external to the model and can be updated instantly without retraining.

47
00:03:08,640 --> 00:03:12,960
 Documents can be added, removed, or corrected in real time.

48
00:03:13,600 --> 00:03:17,280
 Every response can be traced back to the exact sources used,

49
00:03:17,280 --> 00:03:19,520
 improving transparency and trust.

50
00:03:19,520 --> 00:03:23,920
 In production systems, Rag is not a workaround.

51
00:03:23,920 --> 00:03:25,440
 It's the correct design choice.

52
00:03:25,440 --> 00:03:30,000
 It separates intelligence from knowledge, enabling scalable,

53
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:32,800
 maintainable, and auditable AI systems.

54
00:03:32,800 --> 00:03:36,160
 Pollucinations are not bugs.

55
00:03:36,160 --> 00:03:39,760
 They are a direct consequence of how language models work.

56
00:03:40,480 --> 00:03:45,280
 LLMs are trained to predict the most likely next token, not to verify truth.

57
00:03:45,280 --> 00:03:50,240
 When they encounter uncertainty, they fill the gap with confident sounding guesses.

58
00:03:50,240 --> 00:03:54,400
 This becomes especially dangerous in high-stakes domains.

59
00:03:54,400 --> 00:03:59,120
 In legal systems, hallucinated case law can lead to malpractice.

60
00:03:59,120 --> 00:04:03,840
 In healthcare, fabricated drug interactions can put lives at risk.

61
00:04:03,840 --> 00:04:09,280
 In finance, invented compliance rules can result in regulatory violations.

62
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:15,280
 Rag dramatically reduces hallucination risk by grounding responses in retrieved evidence.

63
00:04:15,280 --> 00:04:19,040
 The model is no longer inventing answers from memory.

64
00:04:19,040 --> 00:04:22,720
 It is reasoning over real documents supplied at query time.

65
00:04:22,720 --> 00:04:28,480
 This shifts the model's role from creative generator to contextual explainer.

66
00:04:28,480 --> 00:04:32,000
 By forcing the model to rely on external evidence,

67
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:36,160
 Rag enables verification, citation, and human oversight.

68
00:04:36,800 --> 00:04:41,520
 This is why Rag is a safety mechanism, not just an accuracy enhancement.

69
00:04:41,520 --> 00:04:48,880
 Traditional LLMs operate on static knowledge, snapshots of the world captured during training.

70
00:04:48,880 --> 00:04:55,440
 This includes historical web data, archive documentation, and frozen data sets.

71
00:04:55,440 --> 00:05:02,240
 While useful for general reasoning, static knowledge quickly becomes outdated in real organizations.

72
00:05:02,960 --> 00:05:08,080
 Businesses operate on living data, databases update continuously,

73
00:05:08,080 --> 00:05:15,280
 APIs reflect real-time states, internal documents evolve daily, customer interactions generate

74
00:05:15,280 --> 00:05:22,160
 constant new signals. This dynamic data is where business value lives, and where AI must operate

75
00:05:22,160 --> 00:05:28,560
 accurately. Rag bridges this gap by connecting language models to dynamic data sources.

76
00:05:29,200 --> 00:05:33,680
 Instead of relying on stale knowledge, the model retrieves the most relevant,

77
00:05:33,680 --> 00:05:36,480
 current information at the moment a question is asked.

78
00:05:36,480 --> 00:05:42,320
 This ensures that responses reflect what is true now, not what was true during training.

79
00:05:42,320 --> 00:05:47,040
 Without Rag, AI systems remain disconnected from reality.

80
00:05:47,040 --> 00:05:53,520
 With Rag, they become operationally useful. At a technical level, Rag follows a simple,

81
00:05:53,520 --> 00:06:00,240
 but powerful workflow. First, the system retrieves relevant documents from your knowledge sources

82
00:06:00,240 --> 00:06:04,880
 based on the user's query. These sources might include databases,

83
00:06:04,880 --> 00:06:12,240
 document stores, wikis, or APIs. Next, the retrieved content is injected into the prompt

84
00:06:12,240 --> 00:06:20,320
 as context. This step grounds the model with concrete evidence. Finally, the model generates a response

85
00:06:20,320 --> 00:06:26,960
 based on that evidence, not on its internal memory. The critical insight is that your data

86
00:06:26,960 --> 00:06:32,880
 remains external to the model. It lives in searchable systems that can be updated independently.

87
00:06:32,880 --> 00:06:40,080
 At query time, only the most relevant information is provided, keeping prompt sufficient and

88
00:06:40,080 --> 00:06:46,800
 responses accurate. This architecture improves traceability, since every answer can be linked

89
00:06:46,800 --> 00:06:53,680
 back to specific source documents. It also simplifies governance, auditing, and compliance.

90
00:06:53,680 --> 00:06:59,280
 Key requirements for production AI systems, traditional search systems,

91
00:06:59,280 --> 00:07:05,360
 return lists of documents. They leave the hard work, reading, synthesizing, and reasoning

92
00:07:05,360 --> 00:07:12,960
 to humans. Rag goes much further. Rag retrieves documents, understands their content,

93
00:07:12,960 --> 00:07:18,880
 synthesizes insights across multiple sources, and explains results in natural language.

94
00:07:18,880 --> 00:07:26,240
 This transforms static documentation into interactive expertise. Instead of asking,

95
00:07:26,240 --> 00:07:32,720
 where is this information? Users can ask, what does this mean? Or how does this apply to my

96
00:07:32,720 --> 00:07:40,160
 situation? The system doesn't just point, it explains. This capability enables conversational

97
00:07:40,160 --> 00:07:46,240
 access to complex knowledge bases, turning AI into a reasoning layer on top of your data.

98
00:07:46,240 --> 00:07:52,720
 That's why Rag is not just better search. It's a fundamentally different way of interacting with

99
00:07:52,720 --> 00:07:59,520
 information. Rag becomes essential in enterprise scenarios where accuracy and traceability matter.

100
00:07:59,520 --> 00:08:06,640
 Internal knowledge bases benefit by turning fragmented documentation into a single conversational

101
00:08:06,640 --> 00:08:13,520
 interface. Customer support systems rely on Rag to provide accurate, up-to-date answers,

102
00:08:13,520 --> 00:08:19,920
 grounded in official documentation, and ticket history. In compliance and policy systems,

103
00:08:19,920 --> 00:08:26,800
 Rag ensures responses are always sourced from current, approved documents. Product documentation

104
00:08:26,800 --> 00:08:34,720
 assistance use Rag to navigate complex APIs and technical guides. Analytics platforms use Rag

105
00:08:34,720 --> 00:08:41,200
 to explain live metrics and trends tied directly to data pipelines. In all these cases,

106
00:08:41,200 --> 00:08:48,080
 the common requirement is trust. Stakeholders need confidence that AI responses reflect reality.

107
00:08:48,080 --> 00:08:56,080
 Rag provides that foundation by tying every answer back to real sources, not using Rag,

108
00:08:56,080 --> 00:09:02,320
 introduces serious risks. Systems provide outdated answers that no longer reflect reality.

109
00:09:02,880 --> 00:09:09,280
 Silent hallucinations slip through undetected, spreading misinformation. Trust erodes quickly

110
00:09:09,280 --> 00:09:15,760
 once users realize the AI is unreliable. Teams are then forced into manual verification,

111
00:09:15,760 --> 00:09:21,520
 negating productivity gains. Eventually, business units stop using the system altogether.

112
00:09:21,520 --> 00:09:28,560
 AI initiatives fail, not because the technology is weak, but because it wasn't grounded correctly.

113
00:09:29,200 --> 00:09:33,520
 RAG prevents these failures by ensuring answers are current,

114
00:09:33,520 --> 00:09:38,720
 verifiable, and traceable. In production, the cost of not using Rag

115
00:09:38,720 --> 00:09:46,240
 is far higher than the cost of implementing it. Rag should be treated as foundational infrastructure,

116
00:09:46,240 --> 00:09:53,200
 not an optional enhancement. It establishes a clean separation between knowledge and intelligence,

117
00:09:53,760 --> 00:10:00,960
 making systems safer, easier to update, and more auditable. With Rag, knowledge updates happen

118
00:10:00,960 --> 00:10:07,920
 instantly without retraining. Responses are grounded in verifiable sources. Compliance teams can

119
00:10:07,920 --> 00:10:15,280
 audit outputs. Engineers can debug failures. Stakeholders can trust results. As you move from

120
00:10:15,280 --> 00:10:22,240
 demos to production, Rag is what allows AI systems to scale responsibly. It's the architectural

121
00:10:22,240 --> 00:10:28,400
 pattern that turns impressive prototypes into business critical systems. In modern enterprise

122
00:10:28,400 --> 00:10:35,200
 AI, Rag is not a nice to have. It is the minimum standard for trustworthy AI.

